{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Your daughter-in-law Tamar has played the harlot.</b> Why was it necessary to say afterward, ״and also, behold, she is with child by harlotry״? Perhaps one may say that in that period they would not judge [a case of] harlotry without pregnancy. Another explanation: they would not judge unless she committed harlotry with two men." "<b>Bring her out, and let her be burnt.</b> Ephraim [ha-Maksha'ah] raises the difficulty: Tamar was the daughter of Shem, who was a priest, and a priest's daughter who commits harlotry is [punished] by burning. But the question arises: there were no witnesses and no warning. And R. Joseph of the Land of Israel resolved it: it was a licentious generation, therefore they obligated her in order to make a safeguard for the Torah, as we say in Sanhedrin, chapter נגמר הדין: it is taught, R. Elijah b. R. Yose says: a court may punish and flog not according to law, etc., and so Joshua, who killed Achan, and the Talmud concludes that he did not do so as a legal act but in order to subjugate Israel. Yet it remains difficult: since she was the daughter of Shem, then what is this that is written earlier, ״remain a widow in your father's house״? For when you reckon the years of Shem you will find that he had already died. And there in tractate Sotah it indeed seems so, for it concludes that she said to him: I am an orphan, I am unmarried. And it appears to me that even after her father's death it is still called her father's house."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:41.236836584Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}